Done. Here's what I built:

`/risk validate` — the second half of the prediction validation loop. It:

1. **Loads the most recent snapshot** from `.yoyo/risk_snapshots.jsonl (saved by /risk snapshot`)
2. **Parses git history** since the snapshot using `git log <hash>..HEAD --name-only --oneline`
3. Classifies commits as "revert" or "fix" by scanning commit messages, collecting all files touched in those commits as "broke" files
4. Computes Precision@10 (how many of our top-10 predictions actually had issues) and Recall@10 (what fraction of all broken files we caught)
5. Reports surprises — files that broke but weren't in the prediction, with their current risk rank for context

New functions:
• parse_git_log_name_only() — parses git log output into structured commit entries
• classify_broke_files() — identifies files that appeared in revert/fix commits
• `compute_validation()` — compares predictions against actual breakage
• format_validation_report() — formats the human-readable report

**Tests added** (11 new tests):
• Git log parsing (basic, no trailing blank)
• Commit classification (revert, fix, empty)
• Validation computation (perfect prediction, partial, no breakage)
• Report formatting (key sections present, no-surprises case)
• Routing test for the new subcommand

Help text updated to document `snapshot and validate` subcommands.
